WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.633
Module 10.

00:01.983 --> 00:05.072
Getting value safely: the AI use-case playbook.

00:05.421 --> 00:07.976
AI governance is not about avoiding AI.

00:08.326 --> 00:12.517
This module maps common legal tasks to the value AI can add

00:12.517 --> 00:16.708
and the controls each requires, so that teams can adopt AI confidently.

00:18.458 --> 00:22.289
By the end of this module you will be able to: identify

00:22.289 --> 00:26.121
high-value, low-risk uses in your practice area; match each use case to

00:26.121 --> 00:29.952
a review tier; plan a safe pilot for a new AI use.

00:31.702 --> 00:33.316
Here is a practical playbook.

00:33.666 --> 00:36.718
Summarising documents you provide is high value and

00:36.718 --> 00:39.389
low risk, review tier one or two.

00:39.739 --> 00:43.385
First drafts of letters and clauses, issue-spotting in a contract

00:43.385 --> 00:47.030
and building a chronology from supplied records are all high

00:47.030 --> 00:50.676
value with medium risk, so they need tier two review.

00:51.026 --> 00:55.821
A research memo built on authority you supplied is medium value and medium risk.

00:56.171 --> 00:59.935
Finding authority from a general model's memory is low value and

00:59.935 --> 01:03.357
high risk, so avoid it and use proper research tools.

01:03.707 --> 01:08.491
Court filings can benefit from AI drafting, but they are always tier three.

01:08.841 --> 01:11.534
And deadline and limitation calculations should not be

01:11.534 --> 01:14.228
done by a general AI tool at all.

01:14.578 --> 01:16.226
Use your docketing system.

01:17.976 --> 01:22.109
When your team wants to adopt a new AI use, run a safe pilot.

01:22.459 --> 01:26.546
Pick one repetitive, well-understood task with a clear right answer.

01:26.896 --> 01:29.514
Have the Governance Lead approve the tool, register

01:29.514 --> 01:32.132
it, and confirm its data terms and logging.

01:32.482 --> 01:37.091
Run the AI-assisted process in parallel with your normal process for a few weeks.

01:37.441 --> 01:41.760
Measure the time saved, the errors caught at review and what users think.

01:42.110 --> 01:45.222
Then decide whether to adopt, adjust or stop, and

01:45.222 --> 01:48.333
document the decision in the policy and the training.

01:50.083 --> 01:52.928
That brings us to the purpose of this whole programme.

01:53.278 --> 01:55.530
The goal is not to slow your firm down.

01:55.880 --> 01:58.556
It is to let you use AI confidently, because

01:58.556 --> 02:01.232
the controls are real and you can prove them.

02:01.582 --> 02:03.149
Clarity about the risks.

02:03.499 --> 02:05.241
Control through process.

02:05.591 --> 02:07.298
Confidence for your clients.

02:07.647 --> 02:09.575
Thank you for completing this module.

02:11.325 --> 02:13.101
Let's recap the key points.

02:13.451 --> 02:16.899
Most value is in tasks grounded in documents you supply.

02:17.249 --> 02:19.385
Every use case gets a review tier.

02:19.735 --> 02:23.137
Pilot new uses in parallel, measure, then adopt.

02:24.887 --> 02:26.013
Knowledge check.

02:26.363 --> 02:29.951
Which task is the best first AI pilot for a litigation team?

02:30.301 --> 02:33.760
Option A: Calculating limitation periods.

02:34.110 --> 02:39.857
Option B: Building a chronology from documents the team supplies, with tier 2 review.

02:40.207 --> 02:44.097
Option C: Finding new authority with a general chatbot.

02:44.447 --> 02:47.291
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

02:53.041 --> 02:54.655
The answer is option B.

02:55.005 --> 03:00.311
Grounded, repetitive tasks with clear right answers give value with manageable risk.
